    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what-it-is">
    <h2>2. What DroidHands is (and isn't)</h2>
    <p>
      DroidHands is a user-controlled bridge implementing the Model Context Protocol (MCP) so that AI agents or clients <strong>you</strong> select can invoke tools on <strong>your</strong> mobile device within permissions you configure.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Not an AI provider:</strong> We do not operate, train, host, audit, or control Claude, ChatGPT, Grok, Muse, or any other AI model or MCP client you connect.</li>
      <li><strong>Not a carrier, bank, or messaging platform:</strong> We are not a telecommunications provider, financial institution, or messaging service, and we are not responsible for carrier SMS/voice/data charges or delivery failures.</li>
      <li><strong>No affiliation or endorsement:</strong> DroidHands is an independent project and is not affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by Anthropic, OpenAI, xAI, Meta/WhatsApp, Google, Apple, or any third-party app developer.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="ai-risks">
    <h2>3. AI agent risks &amp; your sole responsibility</h2>
    <p>
      Giving an autonomous AI agent access to your phone is inherently risky. By using DroidHands, you expressly acknowledge and assume all risks associated with AI-driven device control, including without limitation:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hallucinations, errors &amp; unintended actions:</strong> AI agents may misunderstand your instructions, invent facts, choose the wrong contact or app, send embarrassing or harmful messages, place unintended calls, delete or alter data, or initiate purchases or financial transactions.</li>
      <li><strong>Prompt injection &amp; adversarial content:</strong> Text your agent reads from incoming SMS, WhatsApp messages, notifications, emails, calendar invites, websites, or app screens may contain malicious instructions designed to hijack the agent into exfiltrating data or taking unauthorized actions within its grant.</li>
      <li><strong>UI automation fragility:</strong> Screen-reading and UI-control tools (including Android's Accessibility Service) depend on third-party app interfaces that can change without notice, shift due to popups or notifications, or cause taps and keystrokes to land on unintended controls.</li>
      <li><strong>Network interruptions &amp; duplicate actions:</strong> If a connection drops mid-request, an action may still complete on your phone while the agent receives an error and retries it, potentially sending duplicate messages or repeating an operation.</li>
      <li><strong>Best-effort safeguards only:</strong> Features described in our <a href={SECURITY_URL}>Security Model</a> — including app allowlists, the never-allow list, one-time password (OTP) redaction, confirmation prompts, active hours, and rate limits — are <strong>best-effort conveniences, not security guarantees</strong>. For example, OTP redaction cannot catch every code format or codes displayed inside arbitrary app UIs, and the never-allow list relies on heuristics and package lists that may not cover every sensitive app on your phone.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>You are solely and exclusively responsible for:</strong> (a) choosing which AI agents to pair, which OS permissions to grant, and whether to restrict access to “Only what I choose” or enable confirmation prompts; (b) keeping your phone, pairing codes, connection URLs, and self-hosted relays secure; (c) monitoring agent activity and revoking compromised or misbehaving agents; and (d) <strong>every consequence, cost, loss, claim, or liability</strong> resulting from any action performed by an agent connected to your device.
    </p>
  </section>

